<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hundred Days' Reform</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hundred Days Reform, Hundred Days</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hundred Days' Reform was a programme of modernising reforms launched by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> in 1898, covering education, administration and the economy. It was ended after about a hundred days by a conservative coup backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>. Its failure is a key episode in the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform.jpg" alt="Hundred Days' Reform" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._A._P._Martin_%26_faculty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
